spicious optimality.\n\nNow Level 2: Check reasoning validity. The solution uses valid methods: reduction via permutation, explicit construction, lower bound via counting argument. All are valid. There's no invalid method. The solution includes concrete details for the construction: defines R_i and C_j explicitly. So at Level 2, method is valid.\n\nNow Level 3: Presentation quality. The solution is fairly complete. It provides explicit definitions of rectangles, the lower bound argument includes concrete sets B and C. The proof is rigorous. There might be minor gaps: For the lower bound argument, they claim \"For any i∈[a,b] we have i-1≥a-1>d, so the only possible element of B that can lie in T is b_a=(a,a-1).\" Let's verify: If rectangle T = [a,b]×[c,d] with a > d. The set B = {(i,i-1) : i=2..n}. For any i in [a,b], i-1 >= a-1 > d. So column index i-1 > d, which is outside the column interval [c,d], so (i,i-1) cannot be in T. However, the element (a,a-1) has column a-1 > d, so also not in [c,d]? Wait, they claim the only possible element is (a,a-1). But if a-1 > d, then (a,a-1) is also not in the column interval [c,d]. Actually they might have a mistake: For T to contain (a,a-1), we need column a-1 to be within [c,d]. But we have a > d, so a-1 >= d? Let's check: a > d, so a-1 >= d? If a > d, then a-1 >= d? Example: a=5, d=4 => a-1 = 4 = d. So a-1 can be equal to d, which is allowed. So a-1 >= d. But they need a-1 <= d to be in [c,d] (since column interval is [c,d], inclusive). So a-1 <= d. So for a-1 = d, it's okay. For a-1 > d, it's not. So the condition a > d implies a-1 >= d, but not necessarily <= d. Actually a > d implies a-1 >= d? Let's examine: If a > d, then a >= d+1, so a-1 >= d. So a-1 >= d. So a-1 could be equal to d or greater. So (a,a-1) is in T only if a-1 <= d, i.e., a-1 = d. So the only possible element of B in T is (a,a-1) when a-1 = d. So the argument is that at most one element of B can be in T, because any i > a will have i-1 > a-1 >= d, so colu