                "content": "rectangles.{\n  \"solution\": \"### Summary ###\\n\\n**a. Verdict:** I have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is \\\\boxed{2025}.\\n\\n**b. Method Sketch:**\\n- Model the $n\\\\times n$ board (here $n=2025$) as a complete bipartite graph $K_{n,n}$ whose left part represents the rows and right part the columns.  The $n$ uncovered unit squares form a perfect matching $M$ in this graph (one edge incident with each vertex).  \\n- A rectangular tile that does not contain an uncovered square corresponds to a complete bipartite subgraph (a *biclique*) whose vertex sets are a set of rows $R$ and a set of columns $C$ with the property that no edge of $M$ lies inside $R\\\\times C$.  Thus a placement of tiles that covers every non‑hole square exactly once is exactly a partition of the edge set $E(K_{n,n})\\\\setminus M$ into bicliques.\\n- **Lower bound:**  Write the $n\\\\times n$ $0$–$1$ matrix $A$ of the uncovered squares as $A=J-I$, where $J$ is the all‑ones matrix and $I$ the identity matrix (after relabelling rows and columns so that the uncovered squares lie on a perfect matching).  Each biclique contributes a rank‑$1$ $0$–$1$ matrix (the outer product of its row‑indicator vector and column‑indicator vector).  Since ${\\\\rm rank}(J-I)=n$, any decomposition of $J-I$ into $k$ rank‑$1$ matrices satisfies $k\\\\ge n$.  Hence at least $n$ tiles are necessary.\\n- **Upper bound (construction):**  Choose any perfect matching $M$; for concreteness let $M$ consist of the edges $(i,\\\\sigma(i))$ for a permutation $\\\\sigma$ of $\\\\{1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$.  For each column $j$ define the set $R_j=\\\\{i\\\\mid i\\\\neq \\\\sigma^{-1}(j)\\\\}$ and the singleton $C_j=\\\\{j\\\\}$.  The biclique $R_j\\\\times C_j$ contains exactly the $n-1$ edges of column $j$ that are not in $M$, and the $n$ bicliques $\\\\{R_j\\\\times C_j\\\\}_{j=1}^n$ are pairwise disjoint and together cover all edges of $K_{n,n}\\\\setminus M$.  Translating back to the board, for each column $j$ we place a vertical $1\\\\times (n-1)$ tile covering every square of that column except the unique uncovered square $(\\\\sigma^{-1}(j),j)$.  This uses exactly $n$ tiles.\\n- The lower and upper bounds coincide, so the minimum possible number of tiles is $n=2025$.\\n- As a concrete illustration for the smallest non‑trivial size $n=2$, take the uncovered squares at $(1,1)$ and $(2,2)$.  The two required tiles are the vertical $1\\\\times1$ tiles covering $(1,2)$ and $(2,1)$.  This example shows the construction works already for $n=2$.\\n\\n### Detailed Solution ###\\n\\n**1. Reformulation as a bipartite‑graph problem**\\nLet the rows be vertices $R_1,\\\\dots,R_n$ and the columns $C_1,\\\\dots,C_n$.  A unit square $(i,j)$ corresponds to the edge $R_iC_j$ of the complete bipartite graph $K_{n,n}$.  The condition that each row and each column contains exactly one uncovered square means that the set $U$ of uncovered squares forms a perfect matching $M\\\\subseteq E(K_{n,n})$; we write $M=\\\\{R_iC_{\\\\pi(i)}\\\\mid i=1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$ for a permutation $\\\\pi$.\\n\\nA rectangular tile whose sides lie on grid lines and which does not contain any uncovered square corresponds to a biclique $B=R\\\\times C$ with $R\\\\subseteq\\\\{R_1,\\\\dots,R_n\\\\}$, $C\\\\subseteq\\\\{C_1,\\\\dots,C_n\\\\}$ and the property\\n\\\\[R\\\\times C\\\\;\\\\cap\\\\;M=\\\\varnothing.\\\\]\\nIndeed, the tile covers exactly the squares $\\\\{(i,j)\\\\mid R_i\\\\in R,\\\\;C_j\\\\in C\\\\}$, and the condition guarantees that none of them belongs to $U$.\\nThus a placement of tiles that covers each non‑hole square exactly once is precisely a partition of the edge set $E(K_{n,n})\\\\setminus M$ into bicliques.\\n\\n**2. A lower bound via matrix rank**\\nAfter relabelling the columns according to the permutation $\\\\pi$, the adjacency matrix $A$ of the uncovered squares becomes the matrix\\n\\\\[A = J - I,\\\\]\\nwhere $J$ is the $n\\\\times n$ all‑ones matrix and $I$ the identity matrix.  The matrix $B:=J-I$ has rank $n$ over the real numbers (its eigenvalues are $n-1$ with multiplicity $1$ and $-1$ with multiplicity $n-1$).\\n\\nEach biclique $R\\\\times C$ corresponds to the $0$–$1$ matrix $uv^{\\\\top}$, where $u$ is the characteristic column vector of $R$ and $v$ the characteristic row vector of $C$.  Such a matrix has rank $1$.  If $B$ can be written as a Boolean sum of $k$ biclique matrices, then, viewing the sum as an ordinary sum of real matrices (because the bicliques are disjoint), we obtain a decomposition\\n\\\\[B = \\\\sum_{t=1}^{k} u_t v_t^{\\\\top}.\\\\]\\nConsequently\\n\\\\[\\\\operatorname{rank}(B) \\\\le \\\\sum_{t=1}^{k}\\\\operatorname{rank}(u_t v_t^{\\\\top}) = k.\\\\]\\nSince $\\\\operatorname{rank}(B)=n$, we must have $k\\\\ge n$.  Hence any tiling uses at least $n$ tiles.\\n\\n**3. Construction attaining the bound**\\nFix a perfect matching $M=\\\\{R_iC_{\\\\pi(i)}\\\\mid i=1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$ for an arbitrary permutation $\\\\pi$.  For each column $j$ define\\n\\\\[R_j = \\\\{R_i\\\\mid i\\\\neq \\\\pi^{-1}(j)\\\\},\\\\qquad C_j = \\\\{C_j\\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\nThe biclique $B_j = R_j\\\\times C_j$ consists of all edges of column $j$ except the unique edge $R_{\\\\pi^{-1}(j)}C_j$ belonging to $M$.  The $n$ bicliques $B_1,\\\\dots,B_n$ are pairwise edge‑disjoint and their union is exactly $E(K_{n,n})\\\\setminus M$.\\n\\nTranslating back to the board, for each column $j$ we place a vertical tile of size $(n-1)\\\\times 1$ that covers every square of column $j$ except the uncovered square $(\\\\pi^{-1}(j),j)$.  The $n$ tiles are disjoint and together cover all squares that are not uncovered.  Thus a tiling with $n$ tiles exists.\\n\\n**4. Minimality**\\nThe lower bound of Section 2 shows that no tiling can use fewer than $n$ tiles, while the construction of Section 3 uses exactly $n$ tiles.  Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is\\n\\\\[\\\\boxed{n}=2025.\\\\]\\n\\n**5. Small explicit example (the 