ly right of them), the total number $T$ of tiles satisfies\\n\\\\[\\nT\\\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\n---\\n#### 2.  A construction using $2n-2$ tiles\\n\\nTake the permutation $\\\\sigma(i)=i$ (the main diagonal).  Then the uncovered squares are $(i,i)$ for $1\\\\le i\\\\le n$.  Row $1$ has no cell to the left of its hole, and row $n$ has no cell to the right of its hole; all other rows have both a left and a right interval.\\n\\nPlace the following tiles:\\n- For each row $i$ with $2\\\\le i\\\\le n$, place a horizontal $1\\\\times(\\\\,i-1\\\\,)$ tile covering the cells $(i,1),(i,2),\\\\dots,(i,i-1)$.  These $n-1$ tiles cover all squares left of the diagonal.\\n- For each row $i$ with $1\\\\le i\\\\le n-1$, place a horizontal $1\\\\times(n-i)$ tile covering the cells $(i,i+1),(i,i+2),\\\\dots,(i,n)$.  These $n-1$ tiles cover all squares right of the diagonal.\\n\\nThe two tiles in row $1$ and row $n$ reduce to a single tile each because one of the intervals is empty.  Altogether we have $(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2$ tiles, and they are pairwise disjoint and avoid all uncovered squares.\\n\\n---\\n#### 3.  Minimality\\n\\nThe lower bound of $2n-2$ tiles and the explicit construction achieving exactly $2n-2$ tiles together prove that the minimum possible number of tiles is\\n\\\\[\\n2n-2 = 2\\\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\\n\\\\]\\nThus Matilda cannot do better than $4048$ tiles, and $4048$ tiles are sufficient.\\n\\n### Final Answer ###\\nThe minimum number of tiles required is \\\\boxed{4048}.\",\n  \"final_answer\": \"4048\"\n}"
