arallel rectangular tiles whose interiors are pairwise disjoint.\n\n---\n#### 1.  A lower bound of $2n-2$ tiles\n\nFor a row $i$ with $\\sigma(i)>1$ define the cell immediately to the left of the hole\n\\[\nL_i=(i,\\sigma(i)-1).\n\\]\nSince $L_i\\notin U$, it must belong to some tile.  Let $T_i$ be the (unique) tile containing $L_i$.\n\n**Lemma 1.**  $T_i$ is contained entirely in columns $1,\\dots,\\sigma(i)-1$.\n\n*Proof.*  If $T_i$ contained a cell in column $\\sigma(i)$, then because $T_i$ also contains row $i$, it would contain the uncovered square $(i,\\sigma(i))$, which is forbidden.  Hence no column $\\ge\\sigma(i)$ can appear in $T_i$.  \u220e\n\n**Lemma 2.**  If $i\\neq k$ are rows with $\\sigma(i)>1$ and $\\sigma(k)>1$, then $T_i\\neq T_k$.\n\n*Proof.*  Suppose $T_i=T_k=T$.  By Lemma\u202f1, $T$ lies in columns $\\le\\sigma(i)-1$ and also in columns $\\le\\sigma(k)-1$.  Without loss of generality assume $\\sigma(i)<\\sigma(k)$.  Then $\\sigma(i)\\le\\sigma(k)-1$, so column $\\sigma(i)$ lies between $\\sigma(i)-1$ and $\\sigma(k)-1$ and therefore belongs to $T$.  Since $T$ contains row $i$, the uncovered square $(i,\\sigma(i))$ would be inside $T$, a contradiction.  \u220e\n\nExactly one row satisfies $\\sigma(i)=1$, so there are $n-1$ rows with $\\sigma(i)>1$.  By Lemma\u202f2 each of these rows forces a distinct tile, whence at least $n-1$ tiles are required to cover all $L_i$ cells.\n\nNow consider a row $i$ with $\\sigma(i)<n$ and define the cell immediately to the right of the hole\n\\[\nR_i=(i,\\sigma(i)+1).\n\\]\nAn analogous argument (mirroring Lemma\u202f1 and Lemma\u202f2) shows that the tile covering $R_i$ must be contained in columns $\\sigma(i)+1,\\dots,n$, and that two different rows cannot share the same tile for their $R$\u2011cells.  Since exactly one row has $\\sigma(i)=n$, there are again $n-1$ rows with $\\sigma(i)<n$, forcing at least $n-1$ distinct tiles on the right side.\n\n**Lemma 3.**  No tile can simultaneously be counted among the left\u2011side tiles and the right\u2011side tiles.\n\n*Proof.*  Suppose a tile $T$ contains $L_i$ for some row $i$ and $R_j$ for some row $j$.  Then $T$ includes columns $\\sigma(i)-1$ and $\\sigma(j)+1$.  Consequently its column interval contains both $\\sigma(i)$ and $\\sigma(j)$.  If $\\sigma(i)\\le\\sigma(j)+1$, then column $\\sigma(i)$ lies in $T$, and because $T$ also contains row $i$, the uncovered square $(i,\\sigma(i))$ would be inside $T$.  If $\\sigma(j)\\ge\\sigma(i)-1$, then column $\\sigma(j)$ lies in $T$, and $T$ would contain $(j,\\sigma(j))$.  In either case we obtain a contradiction.  Hence such a tile cannot exist.  \u220e\n\nTherefore the total number $T$ of tiles satisfies\n\\[\nT \\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\]\n\n---\n#### 2.  A construction using $2n-2$ tiles\n\nTake the identity permutation $\\sigma(i)=i$ for all $i$.  The uncovered squares are the diagonal cells $(i,i)$.  For each row $i$ with $2\\le i\\le n$ place a horizontal tile covering the cells\n\\[\n(i,1),\\;(i,2),\\dots,(i,i-1),\n\\]\nwhich is a $1\\times(i-1)$ rectangle.  For each row $i$ with $1\\le i\\le n-1$ place a horizontal tile covering the cells\n\\[\n(i,i+1),\\;(i,i+2),\\dots,(i,n),\n\\]\nwhich is a $1\\times(n-i)$ rectangle.\n\nThese tiles are pairwise disjoint because they occupy different rows, and none of them contains a diagonal cell, so the uncovered squares are exactly the required ones.  The first row has no left\u2011hand tile (its left interval is empty) and the last row has no right\u2011hand tile; all other rows have both a left and a right tile.  Hence the total number of tiles is\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\]\nFor $n=2025$ this equals $4048$.\n\n---\n#### 3.  Minimality\n\nWe have proved a universal lower bound of $2n-2$ tiles and exhibited a tiling that uses exactly $2n-2$ tiles.  Consequently the minimum possible number of tiles is\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\n\\]\nThus Matilda cannot achieve the required pattern with fewer than $4048$ tiles, and $4048$ tiles are sufficient.\n\n**Answer:** The minimum number of tiles required is \\boxed{4048}."
