"### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:** I have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is \\boxed{4048}.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**\n- *Lower bound:* Use a counting argument on the interior grid lines. For each of the $n-1$ vertical lines between columns $j$ and $j+1$ there must be a tile whose **left side** lies on that line, because the square immediately to the right of the line in the row containing the uncovered square of column $j$ is covered and any tile covering it cannot extend to column $j$. An analogous argument shows that each of the $n-1$ horizontal lines between rows $i$ and $i+1$ must be the **top side** of some tile. A single tile can be responsible for at most one vertical line and at most one horizontal line, therefore at least $2(n-1)$ tiles are necessary.\n- *Upper bound:* Give an explicit tiling that uses exactly $2n-2$ tiles. Choose the uncovered squares to be the main diagonal, i.e. the permutation $p(i)=i$. For each $i=1,\\dots,n-1$ place a horizontal rectangle covering row $i$ and columns $i+1,\\dots,n$ (the part of the row to the right of the uncovered square). For each $j=2,\\dots,n$ place a vertical rectangle covering column $j$ and rows $j+1,\\dots,n$ (the part of the column below the uncovered square). These $2n-2$ rectangles are pairwise disjoint and together cover every square that is not on the diagonal. Hence $2n-2$ tiles suffice.\n- Since the lower and upper bounds coincide, the minimum possible number of tiles is $2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048$.\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\nLet $n=2025$ and consider the $n\\times n$ grid of unit squares.  For each row $i$ ($1\\le i\\le n$) let $p(i)$ be the column of the unique uncovered square in that row.  The condition that each column also contains exactly one uncovered square means that $p$ is a permutation of $\\{1,\\dots,n\\}$.\n\n#### Lemma 1 (Vertical\u2011line requirement).\\\nFor every $j\\in\\{1,\\dots,n-1\\}$ the vertical grid line $x=j$ (the line between columns $j$ and $j+1$) is the left side of at least one tile.\n\n*Proof.*  Let $i$ be the unique row with $p(i)=j$ (the uncovered square in column $j$ lies at $(i,j)$).  The square $(i,j+1)$ is covered, because its column is $j+1\\neq p(i)$.  Any tile containing $(i,j+1)$ cannot extend to column $j$, otherwise it would also contain the uncovered square $(i,j)$.  Hence the left side of that tile must lie on the line $x=j$.  \u220e\n\n#### Lemma 2 (Horizontal\u2011line requirement).\\\nFor every $i\\in\\{1,\\dots,n-1\\}$ the horizontal grid line $y=i$ (the line between rows $i$ and $i+1$) is the top side of at least one tile.\n\n*Proof.*  Let $j$ be the unique column with $p^{-1}(j)=i$ (the uncovered square in row $i$ lies at $(i,j)$).  The square $(i+1,j)$ is covered.  Any tile containing $(i+1,j)$ cannot extend upward to row $i$, otherwise it would also contain the uncovered square $(i,j)$.  Consequently the top side of that tile must lie on the line $y=i$.  \u220e\n\nFrom Lemma&nbsp;1 we obtain $n-1$ distinct vertical lines that must each be the left side of some tile; from Lemma&nbsp;2 we obtain $n-1$ distinct horizontal lines that must each be the top side of some tile.  A single tile has exactly one left side and exactly one top side, so it can account for **at most one** of the vertical requirements and **at most one** of the horizontal requirements.  Therefore the total number $T$ of tiles satisfies\n\\[\nT\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\]\nThus **any** admissible placement needs at least $2n-2$ tiles.\n\n#### Construction attaining $2n-2$ tiles.\nChoose the permutation $p(i)=i$ (the uncovered squares are the main diagonal squares $(i,i)$).  For each $i=1,\\dots,n-1$ place a horizontal rectangle\n\\[\nR_i^{\\text{h}} = \\{(i,j)\\mid i\\le j\\le n\\}\\setminus\\{(i,i)\\}=\\{(i,j)\\mid i< j\\le n\\},\n\\]\nwhich covers the $n-i$ squares to the right of the uncovered square in row $i$.  For each $j=2,\\dots,n$ place a vertical rectangle\n\\[\nR_j^{\\text{v}} = \\{(i,j)\\mid j\\le i\\le n\\}\\setminus\\{(j,j)\\}=\\{(i,j)\\mid j< i\\le n\\},\n\\]\nwhich covers the $n-j$ squares below the uncovered square in column $j$.\nAll these rectangles are pairwise disjoint because a horizontal rectangle $R_i^{\\text{h}}$ lies entirely in row $i$ while a vertical rectangle $R_j^{\\text{v}}$ lies entirely in column $j$, and the defining inequalities guarantee that they never intersect.  Moreover, every square that is not on the diagonal belongs either to some $R_i^{\\text{h}}$ (if it lies to the right of the diagonal) or to some $R_j^{\\text{v}}$ (if it lies below the diagonal).  Hence the $2n-2$ rectangles cover **exactly** the set of covered squares.\n\nSince we have exhibited a placement with $2n-2$ tiles and proved that no placement can use fewer, the minimum possible number of tiles is\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\nThus the answer to the problem is \\boxed{4048}."
