des a lower bound proof and a construction achieving that bound. So we need to check if there are any arithmetic errors: 2n-2 with n=2025 gives 2*2025 - 2 = 4050 - 2 = 4048. That's correct. No arithmetic errors.\n\nCheck constraint violations: The construction uses horizontal rectangles covering squares to the right of diagonal in each row i, and vertical rectangles covering squares below diagonal in each column j. Are these rectangles valid (rectangular tiles with sides on grid lines)? Let's examine: For each i, horizontal rectangle R_i^h = { (i,j) | i < j <= n }. That's a set of squares in row i from column i+1 to n. That's a rectangle of height 1 (one row) and width n-i. That's a valid rectangle (1×(n-i)). For each j, vertical rectangle R_j^v = { (i,j) | j < i <= n }. That's a set of squares in column j from row j+1 to n. That's a rectangle of width 1 (one column) and height n-j. That's a valid rectangle. Are they pairwise disjoint? Yes, because horizontal rectangles are in distinct rows, vertical rectangles are in distinct columns, and they do not intersect because any square (i,j) with i>j (below diagonal) belongs to vertical rectangle R_j^v (since j < i), but not to any horizontal rectangle because horizontal rectangles are only for rows i, covering columns > i. For i>j, j is not > i, so not in horizontal rectangle. Similarly, squares above diagonal (i<j) belong to horizontal rectangle R_i^h, not vertical rectangle. Diagonal squares are uncovered. So indeed disjoint.\n\nThus the construction satisfies constraints: each tile is a rectangle, sides on grid lines, no overlap, each row and column has exactly one uncovered square (the diagonal). So constraints satisfied.\n\nThus Level 1 passes (no arithmetic errors, no constraint violations). Then Level 1.5: check optimality.\n\nWe need to test small cases (n=3,4,5) using the proposed construction and see if any alternative approach yields fewer tiles. Also check special structure: n=2025 is 45^2 (since 45^2 = 2025). So n is a perfect square. The solution uses a simple diagonal construction. Could there be a better construction using the fact that n is a perfect square? Possibly one could use larger rectangles covering more squares, reducing tile count. The lower bound argument: each vertical line x=j must be left side of at least one tile, each horizontal line y=i must be top side of at least one tile. Each tile can account for at most one vertical and at most one horizontal requirement. So we need at least (n-1)+(n-1) = 2n-2 tiles. This lower bound is general, not dependent on n being a perfect square. So any construction must have at least 2n-2 tiles. The construction achieves exactly that bound, so it's optimal. So the lower bound is tight.\n\nWe need to verify that the lower bound argument is correct. Let's examine Lemma 1: For each vertical line x=j (j=1..n-1), the left side of at least one tile must be on that line. The proof: Let i be the unique row where the uncovered square is at column j (p(i)=j). Then square (i,j+1) is covered (since column j+1 is not the uncovered column for row i). Any tile containing (i,j+1) cannot extend to column j, otherwise it would also contain (i,j) which is uncovered. So the left side of that tile must lie on line x=j. That's correct: tile covering (i,j+1) must have left side at column j+1 or left side at column j? Wait, if tile includes square (i,j+1), its left side could be at column j+1 (i.e., tile starts at column j+1). But then it would not cover (i,j+1)? Actually tile covering a square includes that square fully; the left side of the tile is the leftmost grid line of the rectangle. If a tile includes square (i,j+1), its left side could be at column j+1 (i.e., tile's leftmost column is j+1). But then the tile would not cover column j; that is fine. However, the proof says \"cannot extend to column j, otherwise it would also contain the uncovered square (i,j)\". So the tile could have left side at column j+1, which is not on line x=j.