ctangle lies entirely above or below diagonal.\n\nNow they consider sets A = {(i,i+1)} for i=1..n-1, B = {(i+1,i)} for i=1..n-1. These are just above and below diagonal. They claim a rectangle above diagonal can contain at most one cell of A. Let's examine: Suppose rectangle above diagonal has rows [r1,r2] and columns [c1,c2] with r2 < c1 (since rows < columns). For a cell (i,i+1) to be in rectangle, we need r1 ≤ i ≤ r2 and c1 ≤ i+1 ≤ c2. Since r2 < c1, we have i ≤ r2 < c1 ≤ i+1, so i+1 > i, but we need c1 ≤ i+1. Since c1 > r2 ≥ i, c1 > i. So c1 ≤ i+1 is possible if i+1 ≥ c1. But to have two such cells (i,i+1) and (j,j+1) with i<j, we need both i and j in row interval, and i+1 and j+1 in column interval. Since row interval ends at r2, and column interval starts at c1 > r2, we have i+1 ≤ c2 and j+1 ≤ c2. But i+1 and j+1 are > i and > j respectively. Let's test with small example: n=5, rectangle rows 1-2, columns 4-5. Then r2=2, c1=4. The rectangle includes cells (1,4),(1,5),(2,4),(2,5). It does not include any cell of A (i,i+1) because those are (1,2),(2,3),(3,4),(4,5). Actually (4,5) is in A? No, A includes (i,i+1) for i up to n-1, so (4,5) is in A. But rectangle rows 1-2, columns 4-5 does not include row 4, so (4,5) not included. So indeed rectangle above diagonal cannot contain two cells of A because they'd require rows i and j both in row interval, but i+1 and j+1 both in column interval. Since column interval starts after row interval ends, you cannot have i+1 ≤ c2 and j+1 ≤ c2 while also i and j ≤ r2 < c1 ≤ i+1 and j+1. Actually, let's formalize: Suppose rectangle includes (i,i+1) and (j,j+1) with i<j. Then i ≤ r2 and j ≤ r2, so j ≤ r2. Also i+1 ≥ c1 and j+1 ≥ c1. Since c1 > r2, we have i+1 > r2 and j+1 > r2. So i+1 and j+1 are > r2. But also i+1 ≤ c2 and j+1 ≤ c2. So c2 ≥ j+1 > r2. That's fine. But the crucial part: Since i<j, we have i+1 ≤ j. Actually i+1 ≤ j? If i+1 ≤ j, then i+1 ≤ j ≤ r2, but i+1 > r2 (since c1 > r2 and i+1 ≥ c1). Contradiction. So i+1 > j.