ve better.\n\nThus Level 1.5 passes (optimality check passes). So we proceed to Level 2: Check reasoning validity.\n\nWe need to examine the methods used: bipartite graph representation, matrix rank argument, decomposition into rank-1 matrices, analysis of columns to deduce structure, explicit construction. Are these valid methods? Yes. The solution uses linear algebra (matrix rank) which is a valid method. It uses combinatorial reasoning about bicliques. The argument that each tile corresponds to a rank-1 matrix is valid. The deduction that each column must have exactly one tile missing that column (v_{t_j}=e_j) is a valid reasoning. The construction is explicit. So methods are valid.\n\nThus Level 2 passes.\n\nNow Level 3: Presentation quality. We need to check for justification gaps or critical errors. The solution is fairly detailed, but we need to see if any steps are missing or insufficiently justified.\n\nThe solution includes:\n\n1. Reformulation as bipartite graph. Good.\n\n2. Lower bound via matrix rank. It states eigenvalues of J-I are n-1 (multiplicity 1) and -1 (multiplicity n-1), so rank = n. That's correct. It then says each biclique contributes rank 1. That's valid. Then sum of k rank-1 matrices yields rank ≤ k, so k ≥ n. This is a valid argument.\n\nPotential justification gap: The claim \"Each biclique contributes the matrix uv^T, where u and v are the characteristic vectors of R and C. Such a matrix has rank 1.\" That's correct. No gap.\n\n3. Structure of an optimal tiling: It assumes exactly n tiles and then deduces that each column j must have exactly one tile with v_{t_j,j}=0 and others v_{t,j}=1, and that u_t = all-ones for t ≠ t_j, etc. This reasoning is a bit terse but appears correct. It might be missing a justification for why exactly one index t_j must satisfy v_{t_j,j}=0. It says: \"Since the result contains a single zero entry (in row j) and 1 in all other rows, exactly one index t_j must satisfy v_{t_j,j}=0; for every t≠t_j we have v_{t,j}=1.\" This is based on the sum of characteristic vectors: sum_{t: v_{t,j}=1} u_t = vector of all ones except zero at row j. Since each u_t is a 0-1 vector, to get a zero at row j, we need that row j is missing from all contributions. So at least one tile must have v_{t,j}=0 (i.e., not include column j). Actually, we need exactly one tile to have v_{t,j}=0 because if more than one tile had v_{t,j}=0, then the sum would be missing contributions from those tiles for column j, but we need exactly one zero entry in the column j of the sum. Let's examine: For column j, the sum of u_t for all t with v_{t,j}=1 yields a vector with entries equal to number of tiles covering row i in column j. Since each row i ≠ j must have exactly 1 (since J-I has 1's in all off-diagonal entries). Actually J-I has entry (i,j)=1 for i≠j, and (j,j)=0. So for column j, the sum of u_t for t with v_{t,j}=1 must be vector with entry i = 1 for i≠j, and entry j = 0. Since each u_t is a 0-1 vector, the only way to get entry j = 0 is that no tile with v_{t,j}=1 includes row j, i.e., for all t with v_{t,j}=1, u_{t,j}=0. But u_t is characteristic vector of rows R_t. So we need that for each tile that includes column j, row j is not in its row set. That means each tile that includes column j must have row set not containing j. But we also need that for each i≠j, there is exactly one tile covering (i,j). Since each tile is a rectangle covering all rows in its row set R_t for column j. So to have exactly one covering for each i≠j, we need that each tile that includes column j must have row set = all rows except j (i.e., u_t = all-ones except possibly j). But the solution says that for t≠t_j, u_t = all-ones. Actually they say u_t = 1 for all rows for t≠t_j. Let's check: If u_t = all-ones, then for column j, the sum of u_t for all t with v_{t,j}=1 includes contributions of 1 for row j as well, which would make entry j >0. But they require v_{t_j,j}=0 for exactly one tile, and for all other tiles v_{t,j}=1