"### Summary ###\\n\\n**a. Verdict:** I have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is \\\\boxed{2025}.\\n\\n**b. Method Sketch:**\\n- Model the board as an $n\\\\times n$ ($n=2025$) binary matrix where a $1$ indicates a covered unit square and a $0$ indicates an uncovered one. After a suitable permutation of the columns we may assume that the uncovered squares lie on the main diagonal, so the matrix of covered squares is $J-I$, where $J$ is the all\u2011ones matrix.\\n- **Lower bound:**  $J-I$ has rank $n$ (its eigenvalues are $n-1$ and $-1$, both non\u2011zero).  Each rectangular tile corresponds to a rank\u2011$1$ binary matrix $uv^{\\\\top}$ (the outer product of the characteristic vectors of the rows and columns covered by the tile).  A sum of $k$ rank\u2011$1$ matrices has rank at most $k$, therefore any tiling must use at least $n$ tiles.\\n- **Construction achieving the bound:**  Choose any permutation $\\\\sigma$ of $\\\\{1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$ and declare the squares $(i,\\\\sigma(i))$ to be the uncovered ones.  For each column $j$ place a vertical tile of size $(n-1)\\\\times1$ covering all squares $(i,j)$ with $i\\\\neq\\\\sigma^{-1}(j)$.  The $n$ tiles are disjoint, cover every square that is not uncovered, and each row and each column contains exactly one uncovered square.\\n- Since the lower bound $n$ is attainable, the minimum possible number of tiles equals $n=2025$.\\n\\n### Detailed Solution ###\\n\\n**1. Reformulation as a matrix problem**\\nLet $n=2025$.  Number the rows $1,2,\\\\dots,n$ and the columns $1,2,\\\\dots,n$.  Because each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the set $U$ of uncovered squares forms a *permutation matrix*: there exists a permutation $\\\\sigma$ of $\\\\{1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$ such that\\n\\\\[\\nU=\\\\{(i,\\\\sigma(i))\\\\mid i=1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\nAfter permuting the columns according to $\\\\sigma^{-1}$ (which does not change any combinatorial property we are interested in), we may assume $\\\\sigma$ is the identity.  Hence the uncovered squares are precisely the diagonal cells $(i,i)$.\\nDefine an $n\\\\times n$ binary matrix $A$ by\\n\\\\[\\nA_{ij}=\\\\begin{cases}\\n1,&\\\\text{if the square $(i,j)$ is covered},\\\\\\\\\\n0,&\\\\text{if $(i,j)$ is uncovered}.\\n\\\\end{cases}\\n\\\\]\\nWith the diagonal choice of $U$ we have\\n\\\\[\\nA = J - I,\\n\\\\]\\nwhere $J$ is the $n\\\\times n$ all\u2011ones matrix and $I$ the identity matrix.\\nThe rank of $A$ is invariant under column permutations, so the rank of $J-I$ is the rank of $A$.\\n\\n**2. Computing the rank of $J-I$**\\nThe matrix $J$ has eigenvalues $n$ (with eigenvector $\\\\mathbf{1}=(1,\\\\dots,1)^{\\\\top}$) and $0$ (with multiplicity $n-1$).  Subtracting $I$ shifts each eigenvalue by $-1$, so $J-I$ has eigenvalues $n-1$ (multiplicity $1$) and $-1$ (multiplicity $n-1$).  All eigenvalues are non\u2011zero, therefore $J-I$ is invertible and\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\operatorname{rank}(J-I)=n.\\n\\\\]\\n\\n**3. Each tile yields a rank\u20111 matrix**\\nConsider a rectangular tile $T$ whose set of rows is $R\\\\subseteq\\\\{1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$ and set of columns is $C\\\\subseteq\\\\{1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$.  By definition $T$ covers exactly the squares $(i,j)$ with $i\\\\in R$ and $j\\\\in C$.  Let $u\\\\in\\\\{0,1\\\\}^n$ be the characteristic vector of $R$ (i.e., $u_i=1$ iff $i\\\\in R$) and $v\\\\in\\\\{0,1\\\\}^n$ the characteristic vector of $C$.  The $n\\\\times n$ matrix $M_T$ representing the coverage of $T$ is then\\n\\\\[\\n(M_T)_{ij}=u_i v_j = (uv^{\\\\top})_{ij}.\\n\\\\]\\nThus $M_T$ is the outer product of two binary vectors and has rank $1$.\\n\\n**4. Lower bound on the number of tiles**\\nSuppose a tiling uses $k$ tiles $T_1,\\\\dots,T_k$.  Because the tiles are disjoint and together cover exactly the set of squares represented by $A$, we have the matrix identity\\n\\\\[\\nA = \\\\sum_{t=1}^{k} M_{T_t} = \\\\sum_{t=1}^{k} u_t v_t^{\\\\top},\\n\\\\]\\nwhere each $u_t v_t^{\\\\top}$ has rank $1$.  The rank of a sum of matrices does not exceed the sum of their ranks, hence\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\operatorname{rank}(A) \\\\le \\\\sum_{t=1}^{k} \\\\operatorname{rank}(u_t v_t^{\\\\top}) = k.\\n\\\\]\\nSince $\\\\operatorname{rank}(A)=n$, we obtain the inequality\\n\\\\[\\nk \\\\ge n.\\n\\\\]\\nTherefore any admissible placement must contain at least $n$ tiles.\\n\\n**5. Construction attaining the lower bound**\\nLet $\\\\sigma$ be any permutation of $\\\\{1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$.  Declare the squares $(i,\\\\sigma(i))$ to be the uncovered ones; this satisfies the requirement that each row and each column contains exactly one uncovered square.\\nFor each column $j$ (where $j=1,\\\\dots,n$) place a vertical tile $T_j$ defined by\\n\\\\[\\nR_j = \\\\{ i\\\\mid i\\\\neq \\\\sigma^{-1}(j)\\\\},\\\\qquad C_j = \\\\{j\\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\nIn words, $T_j$ is the $(n-1)\\\\times1$ rectangle consisting of the whole column $j$ except the single uncovered square $(\\\\sigma^{-1}(j),j)$.  The $n$ tiles $T_1,\\\\dots,T_n$ are pairwise disjoint because they occupy distinct columns, and each tile avoids the uncovered square in its column by construction.\\nEvery square that is not one of the uncovered $(i,\\\\sigma(i))$ lies in exactly one column $j$ and in a row $i\\\\neq\\\\sigma^{-1}(j)$, hence it is covered by the unique tile $T_j$.  Consequently the $n$ tiles cover all squares except the prescribed uncovered ones, and each row and each column indeed contains exactly one uncovered square.\\nThus a tiling with $n$ tiles exists.\\n\\n**6. Conclusion**\\nWe have proved a lower bound of $n$ tiles and exhibited a tiling using exactly $n$ tiles.  Hence the minimum possible number of tiles is\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\boxed{2025}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\n### End of Detailed Solution ###\",\n  \"final_answer\": \"2025\"\n}"
